 students. Talk about language: what "had" does in "had already". If later: e6-present-claims (ELA grade 6 unit 3, days 63 to 86) comes later, so the unit teaches its own order-and-audience moves for the recount and the presenting lesson stays in ELA. Mid-unit assessment (one day, about day 15): recount a new school-day event to the teacher. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new short episode (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and name its point of view and the moment the past perfect reaches back; recount to the teacher; write a scene; then a respond day. Out: the full novel study and the narrative essay (ELA). Curio Class writes the episode cards, the frames per level, the model scene and the new episode | First because the ELA novella runs weeks 1 to 7 and the first weeks of middle school are the SI rows' own content: a schedule with several teachers is what every new grade 6 student is living. The talk routine and two of the four models start here | Candidate: California 1.26, all four domains in the unit; PRIME Match.LE, one interpretive item on the new episode | The novella holds a flood and a family's hard choice about moving, and a retold folktale; some students have lived through a disaster or a move they did not choose. The unit works from the book's pages, never from a student's own flood or move; an own scene may use an invented character or a chosen place, always (APPROACH, "Sensitive ground"; the children of war guide). Home language: a scene may be planned in the home language first; narrador and narrator, episodio and episode are cognates. Long-term English learners: the past perfect and point of view on the page, not the talk task. Agent reads: sensitivity; spanish (family note) |
