## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Grade 6 mid-year (week 17) | 4 | 2 | 1 | Grade 6 days 81 and 82, respond day 83. Samples units 1, 2 and 3 on new content: a new short episode (name its point of view), a new short argument (mark the claim and evidence), a new cause account (order the chain), then a talk task heard by the teacher (a recount or a role-circle turn) and a written paragraph. Unit 4's end-of-unit record (days 78 and 79) is carried unscored, not re-collected. Look-fors per entry point; the sheet shows "seen" or "not yet" and feeds the respond day's groups and any move between entry points |
| Grade 6 year-end (week 32) | 8 | 2 | 1 | Grade 6 days 158 and 159, respond day 160 (the finals week). Samples units 4, 5, 6 and 7 on new content: a new weather explanation (name the unseen step), a new informative section (name its central idea), two readings of an old find (say how they differ), a new data set (which claim it supports), then a talk task and a written argument or explanation. Unit 8's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. The year's sheet and the unit 8 record go on to grade 7's teacher |
| Grade 7 mid-year (weeks 17 and 18) | 12 | 2 | 1 | Grade 7 days 85 and 86, respond day 87. Samples units 9, 10 and 11 on new content: a new scene (how setting changes the character), a new comparing text (map it), a new worked percent problem (what each part represents), then a talk task and a written paragraph. Unit 12's end-of-unit record is carried unscored |
| Grade 7 year-end (week 32) | 17 | 2 | 1 | Grade 7 days 158 and 159, respond day 160 (the finals week). Samples units 12 to 16 on new content, one task form per key language use: a process text (order the chain, unit 12), an argument (is the evidence enough, units 13 and 14), a data set (which claim it supports, units 15 and 16), then a talk task and a written argument. Unit 17's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. The year's sheet and the unit 17 record go on to grade 8's teacher |
| Grade 8 mid-year (week 20) | 22 | 2 | 1 | Grade 8 days 98 and 99, respond day 100. Samples units 18 to 21 on new content: a new informative paragraph (trace its idea), a new argument with a gap (name it), a new argument with a counterclaim (name it), a new data table (which claim it supports), then a talk task (a briefing or a led round) and a written section or argument. Unit 22's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. Owned ids sampled: LA Inform, MA Argue, LA Argue, SC Argue |
| Grade 8 year-end (week 32) | 26 | 2 | 1 | Grade 8 days 158 and 159, respond day 160 (the finals week). Samples units 22 to 25 on new content: a new graph (what the slope represents), a new retold tale (what its dialogue reveals), a new council text (order the check), a new editorial (claim and the other side), then a talk task and a written explanation or argument. Unit 26's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. Owned ids sampled: MA Explain, LA Narrate, SS Explain, SS Argue. The year's sheet and the unit 26 record go on to the grade 9 teacher |

**Weeks.** After-core checks do not take a core check day (docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md, "After-core
courses"; for grades 6 to 8 the stagger is by school day, and week 32 is a finals week that may hold every
subject's year-end check). In the calendar the core frames give today (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md), the core
course-level check days before the finals week are: grade 6 ELA 59 and 60, 109 and 110; math 80 and 118; science
90; social studies 71 and 72, 114 and 115; grade 7 ELA 57 and 58, 109 and 110; math 45 and 119; science 67; social
studies 63 and 64, 116 and 117; grade 8 ELA 89 and 90, 125 and 126; math 70 and 116; science 75; social studies 48
and 49, 96 and 97, 145 and 146. So the ELD mid-year checks sit on grade 6 days 81 and 82 (week 17), grade 7 days
85 and 86 (weeks 17 and 18) and grade 8 days 98 and 99 (week 20), each on days no core check holds, and every
year-end check sits in the finals week (days 158 and 159). The mid-year checks are placed where each year's units
break most cleanly between host runs. The costs are stated plainly: each check carries the unit just before it
unscored (its end-of-unit assessment covers it), and each year's last unit (8, 17 and 26) is checked by its own
end-of-unit assessment only, with that record and the teacher's sheet passed on. In grade 8 every owned id but SC
Explain (unit 26) is sampled by a course-level check; SC Explain is checked by unit 26's end-of-unit assessment and
returns in grade 9. `frame.py calendar` lists these weeks beside other after-core checks; the weeks stay until the
lead's calendar pass across the after-core frames (For the lead, item 4).

**How a check runs in 45 minutes.** The interpretive parts (students read the new text, or the teacher reads it
twice for levels 1 and 2) and the writing task go to the whole class at once; the talk task is heard one student
at a time, about two minutes each, while the others write, over the two days. Each student is heard once in a
check. The respond day groups students by form and by entry point (at most four groups; the others rehearse in
pairs from the frame). No scores are stored; the teacher's paper sheet stays with the teacher (promise 2).

