133:   model page each unit builds with the teacher) and each end-of-unit assessment's new content of the same kind,
134:   Curio Class's own and invented for practice, named per unit. No source map: ELD places no base units.
135: - **Paper path.** Every lesson runs on paper, cards and talk. Any tool a unit names (a model-sentence replay tool:
136:   hear and replay the day's model sentence; a word deck) has a paper path beside it (the teacher reads the model
137:   aloud, the cards on the table) and needs no student account. No tool records a student's voice or writing, scores
138:   pronunciation or places a student at a level (APPROACH, "What a tool may and may not do").
139: - **Devices.** None per student. One shared display is optional for the model page, the host's data tables and the
140:   replay tool. No owned code needs technology: models are drawn and labeled on paper, so a paper-path class meets
141:   every id in full.
142: - **Costs.** Printing only: word banks, role and question cards and the four model sheets, reused across a year,
143:   about $4 a unit for a class of 30 (a planning figure, not a quote); no supplies are bought.
144: - **Home languages.** A help, never a ranking (bar item 6): planning and talk in the home language before English,
145:   cognates named (including hidden cognates, which share a root but look different), false friends flagged
146:   (asistir means attend, molestar means bother), and teacher notes on how Spanish does the same thing (other
147:   languages when a reader exists, APPROACH "Home languages"). No student translates for another, for the class or
148:   for a family. Family notes are in English and Spanish.
149: - **Safety.** ELD runs no investigation and no field work. Students talk and write from the host's own data,
150:   records and models; where a teacher repeats a moment from the host (a cart rolled down a ramp, a magnet brought
151:   near a clip), it is the host's own step under the host unit's hazards and controls (the host frame's safety sheet
152:   and its safety read), never a new test, and nothing is heated, tasted or mixed.
153: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md,
154:   kind `sensitivity`, community: multilingual learners and immigrant families), and every Spanish note and family
155:   note gets a `spanish` read (APPROACH, "Sensitive ground", Cory 2026-11-21). Before reading, the agent studies the
156:   APPROACH page's Sensitive ground rules, the federal English learner toolkit (US Department of Education, Office of
157:   English Language Acquisition, free online, https://www.ncela.ed.gov/educator-support/toolkits/english-learner-toolkit),
158:   the National Academies 2017 report on children learning English (free to read online) and, for the units on
159:   floods, hazards, war and leaving home, the National Child Traumatic Stress Network guide for educators of children
160:   of war (free online, https://www.nctsn.org/sites/default/files/resources//children_of_war_guide_for_educators.pdf).
161:   Flagged units name more below: units 1 and 7 (a flood and a choice about moving; natural hazards), unit 2
162:   (punishments and slavery in an old law code), unit 9 (Karuk and Yurok river life), unit 12 (food and the body),
163:   unit 17 (invasion and the end of a state), unit 18 (loss in wartime and a parent living apart), units 20 and 24
164:   (slavery at the founding; Native nations and state power), unit 23 (a myth of seizure and a child shared between
165:   two homes), units 24 and 25 (civics and voting, never a question about status or papers), unit 26 (natural
166:   selection, beliefs respected). The newcomer strand and all family notes get the read before use. No outside human
167:   reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14); the course's checks get the answer-key agent's read.
