300: 
301: **Weeks.** After-core checks do not take a core check day (docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md, "After-core
302: courses"; for grades 6 to 8 the stagger is by school day, and week 32 is a finals week that may hold every
303: subject's year-end check). In the calendar the core frames give today (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md), the core
304: course-level check days before the finals week are: grade 6 ELA 59 and 60, 109 and 110; math 80 and 118; science
305: 90; social studies 71 and 72, 114 and 115; grade 7 ELA 57 and 58, 109 and 110; math 45 and 119; science 67; social
306: studies 63 and 64, 116 and 117; grade 8 ELA 89 and 90, 125 and 126; math 70 and 116; science 75; social studies 48
307: and 49, 96 and 97, 145 and 146. So the ELD mid-year checks sit on grade 6 days 81 and 82 (week 17), grade 7 days
308: 85 and 86 (weeks 17 and 18) and grade 8 days 98 and 99 (week 20), each on days no core check holds, and every
309: year-end check sits in the finals week (days 158 and 159). The mid-year checks are placed where each year's units
310: break most cleanly between host runs. The costs are stated plainly: each check carries the unit just before it
311: unscored (its end-of-unit assessment covers it), and each year's last unit (8, 17 and 26) is checked by its own
312: end-of-unit assessment only, with that record and the teacher's sheet passed on. In grade 8 every owned id but SC
313: Explain (unit 26) is sampled by a course-level check; SC Explain is checked by unit 26's end-of-unit assessment and
314: returns in grade 9. `frame.py calendar` lists these weeks beside other after-core checks; the weeks stay until the
315: lead's calendar pass across the after-core frames (For the lead, item 4).
316: 
317: **How a check runs in 45 minutes.** The interpretive parts (students read the new text, or the teacher reads it
318: twice for levels 1 and 2) and the writing task go to the whole class at once; the talk task is heard one student
319: at a time, about two minutes each, while the others write, over the two days. Each student is heard once in a
320: check. The respond day groups students by form and by entry point (at most four groups; the others rehearse in
321: pairs from the frame). No scores are stored; the teacher's paper sheet stays with the teacher (promise 2).
