PROGRESSION columns dict_keys(['strand', 'grade', 'skill_id', 'standard', 'assumes', 'hands_on', 'model_in', 'model_out', 'words', 'cross_subject', 'notes'])
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-si-narrate', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate', 'assumes': 'g5-si-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g7-si-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'later that day; by then; in first period; I had already; the key moment was', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e6-present-claims (presenting in order to an audience)', 'notes': 'Students recount a school-life event in a new middle school setting (a first week with several teachers, a club meeting) for a peer, with clear time shifts. Entry points (all recount a school-life event in middle school, a first week with several teachers or a club meeting, for a peer, with clear time shifts): levels 1 and 2 with a story map by class period, a word bank and the cloze "In first period ___. Later that day ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Before ___, I had ___. Later that day ___.", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, keeping the point of view steady across periods, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount ordered by time and place; past perfect for an earlier event ("I had already left") and pronouns kept steady (I, we, they); later that day, before and by then. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes. Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language first; the peer hears it in English, and the class word wall shows the Spanish periodo beside period. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 the settings multiply (several teachers, new routines), so time shifts between places are the new step; the grade 5 day-by-day cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-si-inform', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform', 'assumes': 'g5-si-inform', 'hands_on': 'g7-si-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'in this class; first of all; Could you explain; students; routine', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e6-present-claims (telling and describing aloud)', 'notes': 'Students explain a class routine to a new student and ask teachers clarifying questions across several classes. Entry points (all explain a class routine to a new student and ask a teacher a clarifying question in another class): levels 1 and 2 with the routine on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "In this class you ___. First ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "In this class, students ___. Could you explain ___?", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, comparing two classes\' routines, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a routine in sections (arriving, working, leaving); formal register with no "you" in the written version ("students hand in"); first of all, in this class and could you explain. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea. Home language: a new student who shares a home language may hear the routine in it from a buddy first, by choice, then in English; no student is made the class interpreter. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 routines differ from class to class, so informing includes asking each teacher; the grade 5 progress cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-si-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain', 'assumes': 'g5-si-explain', 'hands_on': 'g7-si-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'deadline; due; in order to; if, then; that is why', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students explain how they will meet a deadline across classes and why they planned it that way. Entry points (all explain how they will meet a deadline across classes and why they planned it that way): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a cause chain by day, a word bank and the cloze "I will ___ on ___ so that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "If I ___, then ___. That is why ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming a risk to the plan and what they will do, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a plan with its reasons and an if-then step; "if ___, then ___" and "that is why"; deadline, due and in order to. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with one unseen step (the risk). Home language: students may plan in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers here (asistir means attend, not assist). Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 the plan crosses several classes, so if-then reasoning joins the because; the grade 5 step cards are gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-si-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue', 'assumes': 'g5-si-argue', 'hands_on': 'g7-si-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'however; some people think; What is your reason; weigh; role', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students take part in a structured group decision with roles, asking for and weighing reasons. Entry points (all take part in a structured group decision with roles, asking for and weighing reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and a role card, a word bank and the cloze "I think ___ because ___. What is your reason?", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Some people think ___. However, ___." and the role\'s question card, off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two reasons aloud before the vote, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a structured decision with roles (chair, timekeeper, note taker); "Some people think ___. However, ___" and "according to"; however, weigh and role. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a group may weigh reasons in a shared home language before speaking in English; the teacher names the cognate argumento and argument. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 roles structure the decision, so asking for a reason is a job, not a favor; the grade 5 case frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-la-narrate', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-la-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g7-la-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'point of view; had already; later that night; episode; narrator', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e6-plot-episodes (reading literature, same text)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one episode of the ELA unit\'s novel: how the narrator\'s pronouns hold the point of view and the past perfect reaches back. They map the novel\'s episodes, then build a narrative scene with dialogue and a steady point of view, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA novel\'s episodes and write a scene with dialogue and a steady point of view): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of episodes, a word bank and the cloze "I ___. Before that, I had ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with point-of-view frames and transition frames ("Later that night, ___."), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining one point-of-view choice, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a scene told from one point of view; past perfect for an earlier event and point-of-view pronouns kept steady; later that night, had already and transition. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; narrador and narrator are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 point of view and episodes are the target; texts are grade 6 literature with reading support; in grade 8, the owner year, the point-of-view frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-la-inform', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-la-inform', 'hands_on': 'g7-la-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'central idea; can be divided into; types of; section; formal style', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e6-section-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e6-pronouns (forms of English)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one section of the ELA unit text: how its heading and a classifying sentence ("can be divided into") build the central idea. They map the sections, then build an informative section with a heading, a definition and transitions, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA text\'s sections build the central idea and write a section with a heading, a definition and transitions): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of sections, a word bank and the cloze "The central idea is ___. One section ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "___ can be divided into ___. The first type is ___." and heading frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, keeping a formal style with no "you", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a section with a heading that builds a central idea; classifying ("types of", "can be divided into") and a formal register with no "you"; section transitions and central idea. Home language: a student may plan the section in the home language first; clasificar and classify, sección and section are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 sections and formal style are the target; texts are grade 6 with reading support; in grade 8 the classifying cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-la-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-la-argue', 'hands_on': 'g7-la-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'claims; according to; the author states; cite; evidence', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e6-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e6-trace-argument (reading reasons and evidence)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the writer cites evidence with "according to" and a reporting verb. They trace the writer\'s argument on a claim chart, then build one argument paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace an ELA writer\'s argument and write a paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The writer claims ___. According to ___, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with citation frames ("The author states that ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering one counterclaim in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an argument paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence; reporting verbs (claims, states) and "according to"; cite, evidence and claims. Home language: a student may plan the argument in the home language first; citar and cite are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 cited evidence and claim charts are the target; in grade 8 the citation cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ma', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-ma-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-ma-explain', 'hands_on': 'g7-ma-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'ratio; rate; for every; unit rate; is divided by; represents', 'cross_subject': 'math:m6-ratio-parts-problems (ratio problems); math:m6-one-step-equations (equations explained)', 'notes': 'Students explain how they solved ratio problems and one-step equations in the math unit, using tables and tape diagrams. Entry points (all explain how they solved a ratio problem or a one-step equation, using a table or tape diagram): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled table or tape diagram, a word bank and the cloze "For every ___, there are ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "If ___, then ___. The variable represents ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it, with precise terms, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8, the owner year, the "for every" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explained in steps; passive voice for what is done to a number ("both sides are divided by 3") and if-then; ratio, rate and for every. Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; razón is the Spanish for ratio as well as reason, which the teacher names. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations use ratio and variable language; in grade 8 the "for every" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ma', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-ma-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-ma-argue', 'hands_on': 'g7-ma-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'equivalent; counterexample; always; sometimes; however; claims that', 'cross_subject': 'math:m6-equivalent-expressions (equivalence claims); math:m6-statistical-questions (statistical questions)', 'notes': 'Students argue whether two expressions are equivalent and whether a question is statistical, giving reasons and an example. Entry points (all argue whether two expressions are equivalent and whether a question is statistical, giving reasons and an example): levels 1 and 2 with the expressions on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "They are equivalent because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Some people think ___. However, ___. A counterexample is ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution using conditions and definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with a reason, an example and an answer to another view; "Some people think ___. However, ___" and reporting verbs; counterexample, always and sometimes. Home language: a student may test examples in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (expresión and expression, estadística and statistical). Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims are checked with examples; in grade 8 the equivalence cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'sc', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-sc-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-sc-explain', 'hands_on': 'g7-sc-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'is heated; evaporation; condensation; if, then; air mass; mechanism', 'cross_subject': 'science:s6-water-cycle-energy (host investigation); science:s6-air-masses-weather (host investigation)', 'notes': 'Students explain how energy drives the water cycle and how air masses make weather, using the science unit\'s models. Entry points (all explain how energy drives the water cycle and how air masses make weather, using the science unit\'s models): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "Water is heated, so ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "When water is heated, it ___. If ___, then ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming the mechanism and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the "is heated" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a mechanism in stages; passive voice for processes ("water is heated") and nominalization (evaporation, condensation); if, then and air mass. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; evaporación and evaporation, condensación and condensation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations name the mechanism; in grade 8 the "is heated" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'sc', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-sc-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-sc-argue', 'hands_on': 'g7-sc-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'according to the data; fossil; however; counterclaim; supports; claims', 'cross_subject': 'science:s6-plate-evidence (host investigation); science:s6-particle-temperature (host investigation)', 'notes': 'Students argue from fossil and rock evidence that plates have moved and about how particle motion relates to temperature. Entry points (all argue from fossil and rock evidence that plates have moved, and how particle motion relates to temperature): levels 1 and 2 with the data sets on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The fossils show ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to the data, ___. Some people think ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another claim and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the "fossils show" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with a claim, data and a counterclaim answered; reporting verbs and "according to"; fossil, supports and counterclaim. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; fósil and fossil are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims use data sets; in grade 8 the "fossils show" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-ss-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-ss-explain', 'hands_on': 'g7-ss-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'flood; surplus; specialization; led to; was watered; if, then', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss6-egypt-nile (host unit); social-studies:ss6-surplus-specialization (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students explain how the Nile shaped Egyptian life and how surplus led to specialization, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain how the Nile shaped Egyptian life and how surplus led to specialization, saying which cause mattered more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "The Nile flooded, so ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "When the river flooded, ___. If ___, then ___. This led to ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing two causes and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8, the owner year, the flood cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that links geography to society; passive voice ("the land was watered") and nominalization (specialization, surplus); led to, if and then. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; especialización and specialization are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations link geography and society; in grade 8 the flood cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '6', 'skill_id': 'g6-ss-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g5-ss-argue', 'hands_on': 'g7-ss-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'interpretation; historians; according to; however; claims; perspective', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss6-changing-interpretations (host unit); social-studies:ss6-research-contribution (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students argue how and why interpretations of the past change and present a research claim about a contribution, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all argue how and why interpretations of the past change and present a research claim about a contribution, citing sources): levels 1 and 2 with two interpretations on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "Historians used to think ___. Now ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to ___, ___. However, ___ claims ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the "used to think" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument about an interpretation with another view answered; reporting verbs and "according to"; interpretation, historians and perspective. Home language: a student may read a source\'s summary in the home language first if the school has one; interpretación and interpretation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims cite sources; in grade 8 the "used to think" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-si-narrate', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate', 'assumes': 'g6-si-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g8-si-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'during the lab; while; my teammate; had already; by then', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e7-present-salient (presenting in order to an audience)', 'notes': 'Students recount what happened in a lab or group session for a teammate who missed it, keeping each member\'s part straight. Entry points (all recount a lab or group session for a teammate who missed it, keeping each member\'s part straight): levels 1 and 2 with the group\'s role list on a story map, a word bank and the cloze "First ___ measured ___. Then ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "While ___ was ___, ___ had already ___.", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing varied speech verbs for what each member said, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount that tracks several people at once; past progressive with past perfect ("while she was timing, he had already poured") and pronouns kept clear; while, during the lab and my teammate. Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language first; laboratorio and lab, experimento and experiment are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 group roles and lab routines add several people to one recount, so pronouns must stay clear; the grade 6 period-by-period map is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-si-inform', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform', 'assumes': 'g6-si-inform', 'hands_on': 'g8-si-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'materials; can be divided into; each member will; in summary; role', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e7-present-salient (telling and describing aloud)', 'notes': 'Students report a lab group\'s plan with roles and materials and summarize another group\'s plan. Entry points (all report a lab group\'s plan with roles and materials and sum up another group\'s plan): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a text map with materials pictured, a word bank and the cloze "We need ___. ___ will ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The materials are ___. The roles can be divided into ___.", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up the other group\'s plan and naming one difference, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan report in sections (materials, roles, steps); classifying ("can be divided into") and formal register ("each member will"); materials, the roles are and in summary. Home language: a group may plan in a shared home language first; materiales and materials are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the plan has materials and roles, so classifying joins reporting; the grade 6 routine frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-si-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain', 'assumes': 'g6-si-explain', 'hands_on': 'g8-si-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'procedure; is heated; so that; if we skip; Why did you', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students explain a group\'s procedure and the reason for each step, and ask "why" questions of another group. Entry points (all explain the group\'s procedure and the reason for each step, and ask "why" of another group): levels 1 and 2 with the procedure on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "We ___ so that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The water is heated so that ___. If we skip this step, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, asking another group a "why" question about one step, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a procedure explained step by step with a reason for each; passive voice for steps ("the water is heated") and if-then for what goes wrong; so that, if we skip and procedure. Home language: groups may talk the procedure through in a shared home language first; procedimiento and procedure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the explanation is of a set procedure, so the passive voice appears; the grade 6 deadline frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-si-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue', 'assumes': 'g6-si-argue', 'hands_on': 'g8-si-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'claims that; on the other hand; according to; however; question', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students discuss a class issue in a structured debate format, building on and questioning others\' reasons. Entry points (all discuss a class issue in a structured debate, building on and questioning others\' reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both sides, a word bank and the cloze "I agree with ___ because ___. However ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "___ claims that ___. However, ___." and a question card, off before the end-of-unit debate; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, questioning the strongest reason on the other side, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a debate turn with a claim, a reason and a response to the other side; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) and "However, ___"; according to, claims and on the other hand. Home language: a student may prepare the turn in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates debate and debate, opinión and opinion. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the debate format means answering a named speaker\'s reason, so reporting verbs join; the grade 6 role card is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-la-narrate', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-la-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g8-la-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'setting; because of the; had to; tension; muttered', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e7-story-elements-interact (reading literature, same text)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where the setting changes what a character does, and the verbs that carry the change. They trace how setting and character shape the plot, then build a scene where a setting changes what a character does, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how setting shapes a character in an ELA text and write a scene where the setting changes what a character does): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of setting and character, a word bank and the cloze "Because the ___ was ___, ___ had to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The storm had ___, so ___." and dialogue-tag frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining one choice of setting, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 point-of-view frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene where setting drives the action; past perfect for what the setting had done and varied dialogue tags; because of, had to and setting. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate tensión and tension. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 the interaction of story elements is added; in grade 8 the setting cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-la-inform', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-la-inform', 'hands_on': 'g8-la-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'unlike; which means; are both; according to the text; classify', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e7-text-organization (reading informational, same text); ela:e7-phrases-clauses (forms of English)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text that classifies or compares, and the phrases and clauses that carry it ("unlike", "which means"). They map how the text is organized, then build an informative paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA text is organized and write a paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text): levels 1 and 2 with the text map, a word bank and the cloze "___ and ___ are both ___. ___ is different because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Unlike ___, ___. According to the text, ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the order of the comparison, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 classifying cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text; classifying and formal register with phrases and clauses ("unlike", "which means"); section transitions and according to the text. Home language: a student may group the comparison in the home language first; diferente and different, similar and similar are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 organization by classifying and comparing is added; in grade 8 the comparison cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-la-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-la-argue', 'hands_on': 'g8-la-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'some argue; however; enough; sufficient; counterclaim', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e7-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e7-evaluate-argument (reading reasons and evidence)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and judge whether its evidence is enough, marking the reporting verbs and "however". They then build one paragraph that answers a counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all judge whether an ELA text\'s evidence is enough and write a paragraph that answers a counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of claim and evidence, a word bank and the cloze "Some people think ___. However, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The evidence is (not) enough because ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, judging sufficiency in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 citation cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that judges evidence and answers a counterclaim; "Some people think ___. However, ___" and reporting verbs; enough, however and some argue. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; suficiente and sufficient are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 a counterclaim is added; in grade 8 the "Some people think" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ma', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-ma-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-ma-explain', 'hands_on': 'g8-ma-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'proportional; constant; represents; is subtracted from; two-step; for every', 'cross_subject': 'math:m7-proportional-equations (proportional equations); math:m7-two-step-equations (two-step equations)', 'notes': 'Students explain how they wrote and solved proportional and two-step equations from the math unit, saying what each part means. Entry points (all explain how they wrote and solved a proportional or two-step equation, saying what each part means): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled equation and tape diagram, a word bank and the cloze "___ represents ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "First ___ is subtracted from both sides. If ___, then ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining what each symbol represents, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "for every" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that names what each symbol stands for; passive voice for steps and if-then; proportional, constant and represents. Home language: a student may talk through the equation in the home language first; proporcional and proportional, constante and constant are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 students explain what each symbol represents; in grade 8 the "represents" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ma', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-ma-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-ma-argue', 'hands_on': 'g8-ma-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'condition; never; sample; according to the data; argues; however', 'cross_subject': 'math:m7-triangle-conditions (triangle conditions); math:m7-compare-populations (comparing samples)', 'notes': 'Students argue whether three lengths make a triangle and compare two populations from samples, citing the data. Entry points (all argue whether three lengths make a triangle and compare two populations from samples, citing the data): levels 1 and 2 with the strips or data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "They make a triangle because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to the data, ___. However, ___." and a condition frame ("This works only if ___."), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution using conditions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 counterexample cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from a condition or from data, with another view answered; reporting verbs and "according to the data"; condition, never and sample. Home language: a student may test the lengths in the home language first; condición and condition, población and population are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 conditions and data are the evidence; in grade 8 the triangle cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'sc', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-sc-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-sc-explain', 'hands_on': 'g8-sc-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'photosynthesis; is converted; glucose; is taken in; mechanism; energy', 'cross_subject': 'science:s7-photosynthesis (host investigation); science:s7-cell-respiration-growth (host investigation)', 'notes': 'Students explain photosynthesis and how cells use food for energy and growth, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain photosynthesis and how cells use food for energy and growth): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "Light is used to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Carbon dioxide is taken in and ___. If ___, then ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the unseen step and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "is heated" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that follows matter and energy through a process; passive voice ("is converted", "is taken in") and nominalization (photosynthesis, respiration); if, then and glucose. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; fotosíntesis and photosynthesis, glucosa and glucose are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 explanations follow matter and energy; in grade 8 the "light is used" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'sc', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-sc-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-sc-argue', 'hands_on': 'g8-sc-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'population; according to; some argue; reasoning; ecosystem; however', 'cross_subject': 'science:s7-ecosystem-disruption (host investigation); science:s7-body-systems-food (host investigation)', 'notes': 'Students argue how a change to an ecosystem affects its populations and how body systems work together, from evidence. Entry points (all argue how a change to an ecosystem affects its populations and how body systems work together, from evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "When ___ changed, the population ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, linking evidence to claim with reasoning and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "fossils show" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with reasoning that links evidence to claim; reporting verbs and "Some argue that ___. However, ___"; population, argues and reasoning. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; ecosistema and ecosystem are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 reasoning that links evidence to claim is the target; in grade 8 the population cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-ss-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-ss-explain', 'hands_on': 'g8-ss-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'invasion; the main reason was; not only, but also; led to; was spread; trade', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss7-rome-fall (host unit); social-studies:ss7-silk-road-trade (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students explain why Rome fell and how Silk Road trade spread goods and ideas, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain two causes of Rome\'s fall and say which mattered more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain and "One cause was ___. This led to ___. ___ mattered more."; 3 and 4 with the cause chain and frames using "because of", "as a result" and "led to"; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining causes with "not only ... but also" and "the main reason was". The cloze comes off for 1 and 2 before the check (the word bank stays), the frames for 3 and 4 before the end-of-unit writing task and for 5 and 6 after the first application day; the grade 6 flood cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that weighs several causes; passive voice for processes ("the empire was divided") and "not only ... but also"; the main reason was, invasion and was spread. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; invasión and invasion, imperio and empire are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 explanations weigh several causes; in grade 8 the cause-card frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the fall of Rome includes invasion and the movement of peoples; the family note previews it and offers the Silk Road host, and no student is asked to link it to their own family\'s movement.'}
{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '7', 'skill_id': 'g7-ss-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g6-ss-argue', 'hands_on': 'g8-ss-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'compare; whereas; society; argues; according to; however', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss7-comparing-societies (host unit); social-studies:ss7-research-greek (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students compare societies and argue a research claim about ancient Greece, citing sources. Entry points (all compare two societies and argue a research claim about ancient Greece, citing sources): levels 1 and 2 with both societies on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "___ had ___, whereas ___ had ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "used to think" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a comparison claim with sources and another view answered; reporting verbs and "Some argue that ___. However, ___"; whereas, compare and society. Home language: a student may plan the comparison in the home language first; sociedad and society, comparar and compare are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 comparison claims are added; in grade 8 the "whereas" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-si-narrate', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate', 'assumes': 'g7-si-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g9-si-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'last week; before that; added; announced; as a result', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e8-present-reasoning (presenting in order to an audience)', 'notes': 'Students recount a group decision or event for the class newsletter or an announcement, telling it in order for an audience who was not there. Entry points (all recount a group decision or event for the class newsletter or an announcement, in order, for an audience who was not there): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the event, a word bank and the cloze "On ___, our class ___. After that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Last week, our class ___. Before that, we had ___.", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing an opening that hooks a reader and varied tags for quoted speech, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a newsletter recount with a headline, an opening that says what and when, and a close; past perfect for flashback and varied dialogue tags (explained, added, asked); last week, before that and as a result. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes, here the episodes an absent reader needs. Home language: students may draft in the home language first; a family who wants the newsletter in another language asks the school, and no student is asked to translate it. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 the audience is a whole school who were not there, so the opening must say what and when; the grade 7 role list is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-si-inform', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform', 'assumes': 'g7-si-inform', 'hands_on': 'g9-si-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'brief; the main steps are; first of all; any questions; to clarify', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e8-present-reasoning (telling and describing aloud)', 'notes': 'Students brief a group on a task and its steps, and take notes on a classmate\'s briefing. Entry points (all brief a group on a task and its steps, and take notes on a classmate\'s briefing): levels 1 and 2 with the task on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "The main steps are ___. Any questions?", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "First of all, ___. The main steps are ___. To clarify, ___.", off before the end-of-unit briefing; 5 and 6 with the text map only, taking notes on a briefing and asking one question to clarify, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a briefing with a goal, the main steps and a call for questions; formal register and section transitions ("first of all", "to clarify"); brief, the main steps and any questions. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea (the goal of the task). Home language: a student may take notes in any language; the teacher names the cognate clarificar and clarify. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 students lead the briefing and others take notes, so the listener\'s job is written; the grade 7 materials frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-si-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain', 'assumes': 'g7-si-explain', 'hands_on': 'g9-si-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'the disagreement; we agreed to; as a result of; the solution; worked for', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students explain how a group conflict was solved and why the solution worked, without blaming. Entry points (all explain how a group conflict was solved and why the solution worked, without blaming): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of the conflict and solution, a word bank and the cloze "The problem was ___. We agreed to ___, so ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The disagreement was about ___. As a result of ___, ___." and no-blame phrases, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining why the solution worked for everyone, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation from problem to solution to result; nominalization to keep blame off people ("the disagreement", "the solution") and if-then; we agreed to, as a result of and the solution. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step (what each side needed). Home language: students may talk the conflict through in a shared home language first; solución and solution, conflicto and conflict are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 the explanation is of a conflict, so words that name the problem, not the person, are new; the grade 7 procedure frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-si-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue', 'assumes': 'g7-si-argue', 'hands_on': 'g9-si-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'What do you think about; Can you give evidence; to sum up; outcome; respectfully', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students lead a short group decision: invite views, challenge a reason respectfully and sum up the outcome. Entry points (all lead a short group decision: invite views, challenge a reason respectfully and sum up the outcome): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and a leader\'s script, a word bank and the cloze "What do you think about ___? So we decided ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "That is interesting, but ___. Can you give evidence?", off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, summing up both sides fairly before the outcome, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a led decision that opens, invites, challenges and sums up; reporting verbs and "Some people think ___. However, ___"; respectfully, evidence and outcome. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may plan the leader\'s turns in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate respetuosamente and respectfully. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 students lead the exchange, so inviting and summing up are new; the grade 7 debate card is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-la-narrate', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-la-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g9-la-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'flashback; years before; whispered; reveals; incident', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e8-dialogue-incidents (reading literature, same text)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where dialogue and an incident reveal a character, and the dialogue tags that carry tone. They then build a narrative scene with a flashback or rising tension, explaining one craft choice, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all study how dialogue reveals character in an ELA text and write a scene with a flashback or tension, explaining one choice): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the incident, a word bank and the cloze "Years before, ___ had ___. Now ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with flashback frames and dialogue-tag frames ("___ whispered, as if ___."), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining why they used a flashback, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 setting cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene with a flashback or rising tension; past perfect for flashback, point-of-view pronouns kept steady and varied dialogue tags; years before, whispered and reveals. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (molestar means bother, not molest). Owner year for LA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: analyzing narrative craft and writing with point of view, pacing and transitions. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-la-inform', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-la-inform', 'hands_on': 'g9-la-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'furthermore; consequently; central idea; transition; formal style', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e8-paragraph-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e8-verbals-voice-mood (forms of English)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one key paragraph of the ELA unit text: how its sentences build one idea and which transitions move it on. They trace how the paragraph builds the idea, then build an informative section with varied transitions in a formal style, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how a key ELA paragraph builds an idea and write a formal section with varied transitions): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the paragraph, a word bank and the cloze "First, the writer ___. Then ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with transition frames ("Furthermore, ___. As a result, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the structure and keeping a formal style, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 comparison cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a formal section that builds one central idea; classifying, formal register with no "you" and varied section transitions; furthermore, consequently and central idea. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea. Home language: a student may outline the section in the home language first; consecuentemente and consequently are cognates. Owner year for LA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: tracing how a text develops ideas and writing a structured, formal piece.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-la-argue', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-la-argue', 'hands_on': 'g9-la-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'argues that; credible; some argue; counterclaim; according to', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e8-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e8-delineate-argument (reading reasons and evidence)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit\'s argument: how the writer brings in a counterclaim ("Some argue that ... However, ...") and which reporting verbs carry the sources. They then build one paragraph that answers a counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full essay stays in ELA. Entry points (all take apart an ELA argument paragraph and write one that answers a counterclaim from a credible source): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "Some argue that ___. However, ___ states that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with reporting-verb frames ("___ argues that ___.") and a counterclaim frame, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, judging whether a source is credible, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 "Some people think" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph with a claim, a counterclaim and a response; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) with "Some argue that ___. However, ___" and according to; credible, argues and counterclaim. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may read a source\'s summary in the home language first if the school has one; creíble and credible are cognates. Owner year for LA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: evaluating arguments and writing argument with counterclaims.'}
{'strand': 'ma', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-ma-explain', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-ma-explain', 'hands_on': 'g9-ma-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'linear equation; slope; function; is represented by; therefore; if, then', 'cross_subject': 'math:m8-linear-equations (linear equations); math:m8-function-model (functions as models)', 'notes': 'Students explain how they solved linear equations and how a function models a situation in the math unit. Entry points (all explain how they solved a linear equation and how a function models a situation): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled graph and table, a word bank and the cloze "The slope represents ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "If ___, then ___. Therefore, ___ is represented by ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new situation, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 "represents" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of solving and of what a model shows; passive voice ("is represented by") and if-then; slope, function and therefore. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step (why the method works). Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; función and function, ecuación and equation are cognates. Owner year for MA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: following and giving explanations of solving and modeling.'}
{'strand': 'ma', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-ma-argue', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-ma-argue', 'hands_on': 'g9-ma-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'proof; congruent; assumption; this proves; however; counterexample', 'cross_subject': 'math:m8-congruence (congruence claims); math:m8-angle-facts (angle-sum argument from transformations)', 'notes': 'Students explain and defend the transformation argument for the triangle angle sum and argue whether figures are congruent, critiquing another proof. Entry points (all explain and defend the transformation argument for the triangle angle sum and argue whether figures are congruent, critiquing another proof): levels 1 and 2 with the proof diagram on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The figures are congruent because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Some argue that ___. However, ___. This proves ___ because ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another proof using definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 triangle cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a defended proof with a claim, its steps and a critique; reporting verbs and "Some argue that ___. However, ___"; proof, congruent and assumption. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may follow the proof in the home language first; teorema and theorem, congruente and congruent are cognates. Owner year for MA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: hearing and making mathematical arguments with conditions and definitions.'}
{'strand': 'sc', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-sc-explain', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-sc-explain', 'hands_on': 'g9-sc-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'natural selection; is passed on; variation; if, then; force; mass', 'cross_subject': 'science:s8-natural-selection (host investigation); science:s8-forces-motion-mass (host investigation)', 'notes': 'Students explain natural selection and how force and mass change motion, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain natural selection and how force and mass change motion): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "The trait is passed on because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Variation is ___. If ___, then ___ is passed on.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 "light is used" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a mechanism explanation tied to evidence; passive voice ("is passed on") and nominalization (variation, selection); if, then and force. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; selección natural and natural selection, variación and variation are cognates. Owner year for SC 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: mechanism explanations tied to evidence.'}
{'strand': 'sc', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-sc-argue', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-sc-argue', 'hands_on': 'g9-sc-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'kinetic energy; counterclaim; claims; according to; mass; however', 'cross_subject': 'science:s8-energy-transfer-argument (host investigation); science:s8-gravity-mass (host investigation)', 'notes': 'Students argue that energy transferred when kinetic energy changes and that gravity depends on mass, from data, and answer a counterclaim. Entry points (all argue that energy transferred when kinetic energy changed and that gravity depends on mass, from data, answering a counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The data shows that energy ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the counterclaim and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 population cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from data with a counterclaim answered; reporting verbs, "according to" and "Some argue that ___. However, ___"; kinetic energy, claims and counterclaim. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; energía cinética and kinetic energy are cognates. Owner year for SC 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: argument from evidence with a counterclaim.'}
{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-ss-explain', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-ss-explain', 'hands_on': 'g9-ss-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'branch; checks; is divided; federal; power; if, then', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss8-three-branches (host unit); social-studies:ss8-federal-state-powers (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students explain how the three branches check each other and how federal and state powers divide, from the civics unit. Entry points (all explain how the three branches check each other and how federal and state powers divide): levels 1 and 2 with the branches on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "The ___ branch can ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Power is divided so that ___. If ___, then ___ can ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the unseen step (why a check works) and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 cause-card frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a system and how its parts check each other; passive voice ("power is divided") and if-then; branch, federal and checks. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step. Home language: a student may talk through the system in the home language first; federal and federal, legislativo and legislative are cognates. Owner year for SS 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: explaining systems and causes.'}
{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '8', 'skill_id': 'g8-ss-argue', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g7-ss-argue', 'hands_on': 'g9-ss-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'ratification; editorial; argues; some argue; counterclaim; however', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss8-ratification-debate (host unit); social-studies:ss8-editorial-analysis (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students argue a side of the ratification debate and analyze an editorial\'s claim, answering the other side. Entry points (all argue a side of the ratification debate and analyze an editorial\'s claim, answering the other side): levels 1 and 2 with both sides on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The editorial argues ___. Some argue ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "___ argues that ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the other side and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 "whereas" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from sources with the other side answered; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) and "Some argue that ___. However, ___"; ratification, editorial and counterclaim. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may plan the side in the home language first; ratificación and ratification, editorial and editorial are cognates. Owner year for SS 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: argument from sources with another perspective answered.'}

