eval of m5-unit-fraction-division and the other grade 5 fraction rows.', 'Why this unit, here': 'Needs unit 1\'s area work (IM\'s arrow 6.1 to 6.4) and unit 3\'s "how much for one". The volume lessons sit here because they apply fraction division; supporting cluster 6.G.A practising 6.NS.A.', 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled. Kept: fraction-division progression comparison, zero student days. Dropped: additional mixed-number recipe, since retained varied problems include the required meanings and operations.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Food-sharing contexts kept neutral. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '5', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Arithmetic in base ten. Where does the decimal point go, and how do we know?', 'Days': '14', 'Skills': 'm6-decimal-operations; m6-long-division', 'Owned standards': '6.NS.B.3; 6.NS.B.2', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP6, MP8 (the same steps work for every place)', 'What students learn': 'Adding and subtracting decimals with base-ten diagrams, then the algorithm; decimal products by place value and area diagrams; partial quotients tied to the long division algorithm (the grade 6 fluency standard), beginning with manageable multi-digit numbers and then varying the lengths of dividend and divisor, with zeros in the quotient (the grade 5 limit of four-digit dividends and two-digit divisors is not a grade 6 ceiling); dividing decimals by scaling both numbers by a power of ten; estimating first to place the point; money and measurement problems. Fluency is kept up by spaced, mixed practice in units 6 to 8, with no timer.', 'Why this unit, here': 'IM places it after fraction division (arrow 6.4 to 6.5) and before expressions (6.5 to 6.6), so equations and data in units 6 and 8 can use decimals freely. Additional cluster 6.NS.B, kept short.', 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled. Kept: algorithm progression comparison, zero student days. Dropped: box-making extension to protect algorithm teaching and response.', 'Sensitive 