[{'#': '1', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Scale drawings. How can a map or a model be true to something much bigger?', 'Days': '12', 'Skills': 'm7-scale-drawings', 'Owned standards': '7.G.A.1', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP2 (a scale as a relationship), MP6 (units in a scale)', 'What students learn': 'Scaled copies and scale factors; corresponding parts; scaled area grows by the square of the factor; scale drawings with and without units ("1 cm to 2 m" and "1 to 200"); computing actual lengths and areas from a drawing, redrawing at a different scale; maps (IM 7.1.8); corresponding angles compared with a protractor (grade 4 review, IM 7.1.4). Major work practiced: scale factors as constants of proportionality (7.RP.A.2, IM 7.1.2, 3, 5, 6, 8 and 10). Retrieves ratio tables (m6-equivalent-ratios) on day 1. Angle measure and protractor readiness (m4-angle-concepts, m4-protractor) occupies day 1\'s final ten minutes after the hook; day 2 rebuilds them before merged lessons 3 and 4.', 'Why this unit, here': 'IM opens grade 7 here: it starts the proportional strand with pictures before unit 2 names the constant of proportionality. Short, as IM sets it; ratio reasoning is in use throughout.', 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the ratio/geometry Progressions comparison as planning, zero student days. Drop the optional room-drawing and route-mapping extensions; retained scale applications already carry their owned work.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': "Maps of the school or a park, not of a student's home. Paper path: grid paper, ruler, protractor, tracing paper. Sensitivity read."}, {'#': '2', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Introducing proportional relationships. How can you tell if two quantities grow together at the same rate?', 'Days': '21', 'Skills': 'm7-proportional-tables; m7-proportional-equations; m7-proportional-graphs', 'Owned standards': '7.RP.A.2; 7.RP.A.2a; 7.RP.A.2b; 7.RP.A.2c; 7.RP.A.2d', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP2, MP4 (decide whether a real relationship is proportional)', 'What students learn': 'Proportional relationships in tables, the constant of proportionality k and its reciprocal; y = kx written from tables, diagrams and stories; deciding proportional or not from a table (7.RP.A.2a) and from a graph on a line through the origin (7.RP.A.2a); the meaning of (0, 0) and (1, k) on the graph (7.RP.A.2d); comparing relationships across tables, equations and graphs; an investigation with real measurements. Expected error: treating any line as proportional.', 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 1's scale factors and grade 6 rates; prepares students for fractional rates in unit 4. Major cluster, given its full base-text length plus a mid-unit assessment.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the ratio Progressions comparison, zero student days. Keep the different-unit table/graph comparison for 10 minutes within day 30.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Contexts kept neutral. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '3', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Measuring circles. Why is the distance around every circle about 3 times its width?', 'Days': '11', 'Skills': 'm7-circles', 'Owned standards': '7.G.B.4', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP5 (measure with string and rulers), MP8 (the same ratio every time)', 'What students learn': 'Circles, radius, diameter; measuring and graphing circumference against diameter to find pi as the constant of proportionality; C = pi d; arc and part-circle problems; area by cutting into near-parallelograms, A = pi r squared, and the informal derivation of the relationship between circumference and area; choosing between the two formulas. Major work practiced: circumference as a proportional relationship with constant pi (7.RP.A.2, IM 7.3.1, 3 and 7).', 'Why this unit, here': "Uses unit 2's proportional graphs (pi is a constant ratio) and grade 6 polygon area. IM's place. Course-level check 1 follows.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the geometry/ratio Progressions comparison, zero student days. Drop the optional decorative-window and rolling-wheel extensions; day 42 already combines circle measures.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Paper path: string, rulers, paper circles to cut. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '4', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Proportional relationships and percentages. How do we find a fair price after a change?', 'Days': '22', 'Skills': 'm7-unit-rate-fractions; m7-percent-problems', 'Owned standards': '7.RP.A.1; 7.RP.A.3', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP1, MP4', 'What students learn': 'Unit rates with fractions (complex fractions such as 1/2 mile per 1/4 hour); long division of a fraction to a decimal that ends or repeats (IM 7.4.5; 7.NS.A.2d begun here, owned and finished in unit 5); percent increase and decrease with tape diagrams and double number lines, then equations; multistep percent problems of every kind 7.RP.A.3 names: simple interest, tax, markups and markdowns, gratuities and commissions, fees, percent increase and decrease, percent error; measurement error.', 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 2's y = kx and grade 6 percent; IM's chart puts 7.2 before 7.4. Major. Science 7 and social studies 7 lean on its percent.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the ratio Progressions comparison, zero student days. Keep simple interest versus no interest for 15 minutes inside day 62, with a fixed principal and no compounding.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Money contexts stay neutral; no student shares family spending, wages or debt. Tips are a fixed percent of a bill; no judgement of who tips what. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '5', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Rational number arithmetic. What does it mean to add, subtract, multiply and divide signed numbers?', 'Days': '23', 'Skills': 'm7-rational-add-subtract; m7-rational-multiply-divide; m7-decimal-expansion; m7-rational-problems', 'Owned standards': '7.NS.A.1; 7.NS.A.1a; 7.NS.A.1b; 7.NS.A.1c; 7.NS.A.1d; 7.NS.A.2; 7.NS.A.2a; 7.NS.A.2b; 7.NS.A.2c; 7.NS.A.2d; 7.NS.A.3; 7.EE.B.3', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP7 (subtracting is adding the opposite), MP8 (the sign rules from repeated reasoning)', 'What students learn': "Signed numbers on the number line; opposite quantities that combine to 0 (7.NS.A.1a); p + q as a move from p (7.NS.A.1b); subtraction as adding the additive inverse and distance as absolute value of a difference (7.NS.A.1c); properties as strategies (7.NS.A.1d); multiplying with signs, why (-1)(-1) = 1 from the properties (7.NS.A.2a); division with signs and -(p/q) = (-p)/q (7.NS.A.2b); properties for products (7.NS.A.2c); decimal expansions finished on a Curio Class day: why every fraction's decimal ends or repeats, the decimals of negative rational numbers, and an independent long-division check (7.NS.A.2d in full, begun in unit 4 on IM 7.4.5); equations with signed numbers of the forms px = q and x + p = q (IM 7.5.11 and 14 to 16; 7.EE.B.4a begun here, owned in unit 6); real problems with all four operations (7.NS.A.3) and multistep problems with estimation (7.EE.B.3). Retrieves m6-negative-numbers on day 69; the grade 5 fraction rows have readiness on day 68 and rebuilding on day 71.", 'Why this unit, here': "IM's place; signed arithmetic is needed by unit 6's equations. Major, given IM's full length plus a mid-unit assessment. Social studies 7's time-zone unit and science leans fall after its adding and subtracting lessons.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the number-system Progressions comparison, zero student days. Drop reverse decimal-to-fraction conversion until grade 8 owns it.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'IM 7.5.17 (stock market) keeps signed changes as a context with no investing advice; credit and debt are neutral. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '6', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Expressions, equations and inequalities. How can an equation help us find what we do not know?', 'Days': '27', 'Skills': 'm7-two-step-equations; m7-two-step-inequalities; m7-linear-expressions', 'Owned standards': '7.EE.B.4; 7.EE.B.4a; 7.EE.B.4b; 7.EE.A.1; 7.EE.A.2', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP2, MP7 (structure of p(x + q))', 'What students learn': "Tape diagrams and hangers for px + q = r and p(x + q) = r with rational p, q, r; solving fluently by reasoning about the hanger and by algebra, compared with an arithmetic solution (7.EE.B.4a); two-step inequalities, solution sets graphed and read in context (7.EE.B.4b); equivalent linear expressions with rational coefficients: adding, subtracting, factoring and expanding (7.EE.A.1); rewriting an expression to show its meaning, such as a + 0.05a = 1.05a (7.EE.A.2, linking back to unit 4's percent).", 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 5's signed arithmetic and grade 6 equations; a sustained focus on grade 7's major work. Course-level check 2 follows.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the expressions/equations Progressions comparison, zero student days. Keep a supplied fee-and-rate inequality for 10 minutes within day 110.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Contexts kept neutral. Paper path: drawn hangers. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '7', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Angles, triangles and prisms. Which measurements are enough to build a shape?', 'Days': '18', 'Skills': 'm7-angle-relationships; m7-triangle-conditions; m7-cross-sections; m7-area-volume-composite', 'Owned standards': '7.G.B.5; 7.G.A.2; 7.G.A.3; 7.G.B.6', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP5 (choose ruler, protractor, compass, tracing paper or the drawing tool), MP3 (argue a triangle is or is not unique)', 'What students learn': 'Supplementary, complementary, vertical and adjacent angles, with equations for unknown angles (7.G.B.5); drawing shapes with given conditions by freehand, with ruler and protractor, and with technology, the students at the controls (see the course in brief), focused on triangles from three measures: unique, many or none (7.G.A.2); cross sections of right prisms and pyramids (7.G.A.3); volume and surface area of right prisms and composite figures of triangles, quadrilaterals, polygons, cubes and right prisms (7.G.B.6). Major work practiced: two-step equations for unknown angles (7.EE.B.4a); missing-dimension volume equations here are one-step prerequisite work. Day 120 checks m4-additive-angles, m5-shape-hierarchy and m5-volume-additive, with rebuilding on days 122, 126 and 132. Angle measure and protractor use (m4-angle-concepts, m4-protractor), explicitly taught in unit 1, are retrieved on days 121 and 126.', 'Why this unit, here': "Uses unit 6's equations for angles (IM's arrow 7.6 to 7.7). Additional clusters, kept to IM's length.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the geometry Progressions comparison, zero student days. Drop the optional prism-building and room-design projects because retained construction/composite tasks already carry the work.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Paper path: protractor, ruler, compass, tracing paper, clay or paper solids for cross sections. The drawing tool needs no student account; students take turns at the controls on a shared device. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '8', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Probability and sampling. How can a small sample tell us about a whole group?', 'Days': '19', 'Skills': 'm7-chance-probability; m7-probability-models; m7-compound-events; m7-random-samples; m7-compare-populations', 'Owned standards': '7.SP.C.5; 7.SP.C.6; 7.SP.C.7; 7.SP.C.7a; 7.SP.C.7b; 7.SP.C.8; 7.SP.C.8a; 7.SP.C.8b; 7.SP.C.8c; 7.SP.A.1; 7.SP.A.2; 7.SP.B.3; 7.SP.B.4', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP4 (a simulation models a situation), MP3 (argue from samples)', 'What students learn': 'Chance as a number from 0 to 1 (7.SP.C.5); relative frequency approaching probability (7.SP.C.6); uniform and non-uniform models, from reasoning (7.SP.C.7a) and from data (7.SP.C.7b); compound events with lists, tables and tree diagrams (introduced here), sample spaces, and designing a simulation (7.SP.C.8a to c); populations and samples, random sampling, inferences and how much estimates vary (7.SP.A.1, 7.SP.A.2); comparing two populations by center and variability as a multiple of MAD (7.SP.B.3) and by measures of center and variability of random samples (7.SP.B.4). Major work practiced: probabilities and population estimates written as percents and proportions (7.RP.A.3).', 'Why this unit, here': "Supporting and additional clusters; uses unit 4's percent and grade 6 data. IM's last unit. Course-level check 3 follows.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Keep the statistics/probability Progressions comparison, zero student days. Cut the extra information-gap exchange for time; teach both comparison methods from the preceding lesson on day 154, then assess on day 155.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Number cubes, spinners and bags of slips, never betting, cards as gambling or casino framing. Survey questions avoid personal and body topics. Sensitivity read.'}]

math-grade-8
['#', 'Unit and compelling question', 'Days', 'Skills', 'Owned standards', 'C3', 'What students learn', 'Why this unit, here', 'Check set adds', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes']
[{'#': '1', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Rigid transformations and congruence. How can we tell that two figures are the same shape and size?', 'Days': '20', 'Skills': 'm8-rigid-motions; m8-congruence; m8-angle-facts', 'Owned standards': '8.G.A.1; 8.G.A.1a; 8.G.A.1b; 8.G.A.1c; 8.G.A.2; 8.G.A.5', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP5 (tracing paper, grid, ruler), MP3 (argue congruence from a sequence of moves)', 'What students learn': 'Translations, rotations and reflections described precisely; their properties verified experimentally: lines to lines and segments to equal segments (8.G.A.1a), angles to equal angles (8.G.A.1b), parallel lines to parallel lines (8.G.A.1c); moves on a grid with coordinates begun (IM 8.1.5 to 8.1.6, owned in unit 2); congruence as a sequence of rigid motions (8.G.A.2); alternate interior angles and the triangle angle sum argued from transformations; the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two far interior angles, and the angle pairs on a transversal of parallel lines (corresponding, alternate interior, same-side), each argued informally (8.G.A.5 in full with the angle-angle criterion in unit 2; Curio Class adds an exterior-angle activity to IM 8.1.16). Retrieves m7-triangle-conditions; symmetry and signed-coordinate readiness follow the day 1 hook, with rebuilding before use.', 'Why this unit, here': "IM opens grade 8 here: geometric, hands-on, resting on grade 7 scale drawings and triangles. Unit 2's dilations need it.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Geometry progression (grade 8: transformations). Extension: IM 8.9.1 to 8.9.3, Tessellations.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Paper path: tracing paper, grid paper, protractor. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '2', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Dilations, similarity and introducing slope. When are two shapes the same, only bigger?', 'Days': '16', 'Skills': 'm8-dilations-similarity; m8-transformations-coordinates', 'Owned standards': '8.G.A.4; 8.G.A.3', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP7 (similar triangles on a line), MP3', 'What students learn': 'Dilations with a center and a scale factor, on and off the grid; the effect of dilations, translations, rotations and reflections on coordinates (8.G.A.3, begun in unit 1); similarity as a sequence of rigid motions and dilations (8.G.A.4); the angle-angle criterion for similar triangles (finishes 8.G.A.5); slope met as the ratio in similar slope triangles along a line (IM 8.2.10 to 8.2.12, owned in unit 3).', 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 1's moves and grade 7 scale factors (IM's arrow 8.1 to 8.2); its slope triangles start unit 3.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Geometry progression (similarity) and the Expressions and Equations progression (slope). Extension: a dilation with a center outside the figure, drawn and its scale factor checked.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Paper path: grid paper, ruler, a printed center and rays. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '3', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Linear relationships. What does the steepness of a line tell us?', 'Days': '19', 'Skills': 'm8-proportional-compare; m8-slope-similar-triangles; m8-linear-relationships', 'Owned standards': '8.EE.B.5; 8.EE.B.6', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP8 (slope stays the same), MP2', 'What students learn': 'Proportional relationships revisited: unit rate as slope, comparing two relationships shown differently (8.EE.B.5); why slope is the same between any two points, from similar triangles; y = mx for lines through the origin and y = mx + b otherwise (8.EE.B.6); graphs, tables and equations of linear relationships, with negative slope and the vertical intercept in context; the start of function ideas (8.F.A.3 and 8.F.B.4 in use here, owned in unit 6); horizontal and vertical lines; solutions of a linear equation in two variables as the points on its line.', 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 2's slope triangles and grade 7 proportional graphs (IM's chart: 8.2 and 7.2 to 8.3). Major. Unit 4's lines of fit, unit 5's systems and unit 6's functions all rest on it.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Expressions and Equations progression (grade 8: proportional and linear relationships). Extension: two lines with the same slope argued parallel from similar triangles.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': "Contexts kept neutral; savings or pay over time as a context, never a student's own money. Sensitivity read."}, {'#': '4', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Associations in data. Does knowing one thing about a group help predict another?', 'Days': '14', 'Skills': 'm8-scatter-plots; m8-linear-models-data; m8-two-way-tables', 'Owned standards': '8.SP.A.1; 8.SP.A.2; 8.SP.A.3; 8.SP.A.4', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP4 (a line of fit models data), MP2 (slope and intercept in context)', 'What students learn': "Scatter plots of bivariate data, patterns of clustering, outliers, positive or negative and linear or nonlinear association (8.SP.A.1); a student-drawn fitted line judged by closeness of the points (8.SP.A.2), retained on day 61; a linear model's slope and intercept interpreted in context (8.SP.A.3); two-way tables of frequencies and relative frequencies by row or column, and association in categorical data (8.SP.A.4). Two-way tables fall about days 65 to 67. The lessons speak of relationships, lines and the equation of a line y = mx + b from unit 3, not of functions, which unit 6 defines (source map).", 'Why this unit, here': "Moved from IM's sixth place to fourth: IM's chart has 8.6 depending only on 8.3 (and 7.8), so it can follow unit 3 at once, and science 8 leans on scatter plots in base units 8.4 and 8.5 in the same year. Course-level check 1 follows.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Statistics and Probability progression (grade 8: bivariate data). Extension: IM 8.9.4 to 8.9.6, a weather model from plotted data, or IM 8.6.11.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': "Data sets avoid weight, body measures, income and personal topics; public or class-safe data. Polling contexts stay neutral; no student's family vote is asked (ss8-election-info). Paper path: graph paper, a strand of spaghetti or ruler for the line of fit. Sensitivity read."}, {'#': '5', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Linear equations and linear systems. When do two plans cost the same?', 'Days': '21', 'Skills': 'm8-linear-equations; m8-linear-systems', 'Owned standards': '8.EE.C.7; 8.EE.C.7a; 8.EE.C.7b; 8.EE.C.8; 8.EE.C.8a; 8.EE.C.8b; 8.EE.C.8c', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP7 (structure of an equation), MP1', 'What students learn': 'Linear equations with the variable on both sides, keeping equality with the hanger, then by algebra; student-constructed examples of equations with one solution, none or infinitely many, shown by transforming to x = a, a = a or a = b (8.EE.C.7a); rational coefficients, the distributive property and collecting like terms (8.EE.C.7b); systems as the intersection of two lines (8.EE.C.8a); solving systems algebraically, estimating from a graph, and seeing no solution by inspection, such as 3x + 2y = 5 and 3x + 2y = 6 (8.EE.C.8b); real problems leading to two equations (8.EE.C.8c).', 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 3's lines and grade 7 two-step equations (IM's chart: 8.3 to 8.4). Major. Course-level check 2 follows unit 6.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Expressions and Equations progression (grade 8: equations and systems). Extension: a system with no solution posed from two plans and explained from the graph.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Plan and price comparisons kept neutral (two gyms or two phone plans with no brand). Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '6', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Functions and volume. How can one quantity determine another?', 'Days': '23', 'Skills': 'm8-function-define; m8-function-compare; m8-function-model; m8-function-qualitative; m8-volume-curved', 'Owned standards': '8.F.A.1; 8.F.A.2; 8.F.A.3; 8.F.B.4; 8.F.B.5; 8.G.C.9', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP4 (model with a function), MP2', 'What students learn': "Opens by naming unit 4's lines of fit as linear functions; a function as a rule giving exactly one output for each input, its graph as the set of pairs (8.F.A.1); comparing two functions shown differently (8.F.A.2); y = mx + b as the linear functions, with examples that are not linear such as A = s squared (8.F.A.3, begun in unit 3); constructing a linear function from a description or two points, rate of change and initial value (8.F.B.4); describing and sketching graphs qualitatively, increasing, decreasing, linear or not (8.F.B.5); piecewise linear models; volume of cylinders, cones and spheres, and volume as a function of a dimension (8.G.C.9). Teacher note: problems asking for a radius from a volume wait for unit 8's roots, or estimate.", 'Why this unit, here': "Needs unit 3's lines (IM's arrow 8.3 to 8.5) and unit 5's equations; IM pairs volume with functions so the formulas become functions of one dimension. Course-level check 2 follows.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Functions progression (grade 8) and the Geometry progression (volume). Extension: IM 8.5.17 and 8.5.18, scaling one and two dimensions of a cylinder.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Contexts neutral. Paper path: graph paper; cylinders and cones from paper or containers. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '7', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Exponents and scientific notation. How can we write and compare very large and very small numbers?', 'Days': '20', 'Skills': 'm8-integer-exponents; m8-scientific-notation', 'Owned standards': '8.EE.A.1; 8.EE.A.3; 8.EE.A.4', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP7 (exponent rules from structure), MP8', 'What students learn': 'Properties of integer exponents developed from repeated multiplication, zero and negative exponents (8.EE.A.1); powers of ten on the place-value chart; numbers as a single digit times a power of ten and comparing them (8.EE.A.3); operations in scientific notation, choosing units of suitable size, and reading notation from a calculator display (a printed display on the paper path, 8.EE.A.4). Retrieves m6-exponents, explicitly taught and assessed in unit 6 after day 92 readiness.', 'Why this unit, here': "Exponent laws precede scale estimates and operations. Needs grade 7 signed arithmetic for negative exponents; unit 8's roots use its squares. Science 8 uses its scale contexts.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Expressions and Equations progression (exponents, scientific notation). Extension: the ratio of two very large distances written in scientific notation and explained.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Scale-of-space contexts and microscopic sizes; contexts neutral. Sensitivity read.'}, {'#': '8', 'Unit and compelling question': 'Pythagorean theorem and irrational numbers. Is every length a number we can write exactly?', 'Days': '20', 'Skills': 'm8-roots; m8-irrational-numbers; m8-pythagorean-theorem; m8-coordinate-distance', 'Owned standards': '8.EE.A.2; 8.NS.A.1; 8.NS.A.2; 8.G.B.6; 8.G.B.7; 8.G.B.8', 'C3': 'None (math). Practices: MP3 (explain a proof of the theorem), MP6 (exact and approximate values)', 'What students learn': 'Square roots as side lengths of squares, cube roots as edges of cubes; solving x squared = p and x cubed = p, and knowing the square root of 2 is irrational (8.EE.A.2); rational numbers as repeating or terminating decimals and converting repeating decimals to fractions; irrational numbers compared and placed on the number line by rational approximation, and the value of expressions with them estimated, such as pi squared or a sum of square roots (8.NS.A.1, 8.NS.A.2 in full; IM 8.8.10 opens with such an estimate); a proof of the Pythagorean theorem and its converse by area (8.G.B.6); finding unknown sides in two and three dimensions (8.G.B.7); distance between points on the coordinate plane (8.G.B.8). Retrieves m6-four-quadrants taught and assessed in unit 1, and m6-exponents rebuilt and assessed in unit 6.', 'Why this unit, here': "IM's last unit: it needs unit 7's exponents and the grade 7 decimal expansions. Course-level check 3 follows.", 'Check set adds': 'Candidate: Progressions check against the Geometry progression (the Pythagorean theorem) and the Number System progression (irrational numbers). Extension: a second proof of the Pythagorean theorem explained from a drawing.', 'Sensitive topics and teacher notes': 'Paper path: grid paper squares, cut-out proof pieces. Sensitivity read.'}]

