The structural check passes with zero failures. I found two substantive risks it cannot catch: some owned expectations require more than the listed tasks, and grade 7’s sampling task is scheduled before the math host teaches population comparison. I’m checking the exact dates and fixes before writing the report.
exec
"C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command "python -B -X utf8 -c \"import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'scripts'); import frame; s='math-grade-7'; h,rs=frame.table(frame.section(open(frame.path_of(s),encoding='utf-8').read(),'Units')); [print(r['#'],r['What students learn'],r['Why this unit, here']) for r in rs if r['#'] in ['7','8']]; from pathlib import Path; print('MATH DAYS'); [print(str(p)) for p in Path('data/plans').glob('*7*/days.csv')]\"" in S:\CurioClass
 succeeded in 182ms:
7 Supplementary, complementary, vertical and adjacent angles, with equations for unknown angles (7.G.B.5); drawing shapes with given conditions by freehand, with ruler and protractor, and with technology, the students at the controls (see the course in brief), focused on triangles from three measures: unique, many or none (7.G.A.2); cross sections of right prisms and pyramids (7.G.A.3); volume and surface area of right prisms and composite figures of triangles, quadrilaterals, polygons, cubes and right prisms (7.G.B.6). Major work practiced: two-step equations for unknown angles (7.EE.B.4a); missing-dimension volume equations here are one-step prerequisite work. Day 120 checks m4-additive-angles, m5-shape-hierarchy and m5-volume-additive, with rebuilding on days 122, 126 and 132. Angle measure and protractor use (m4-angle-concepts, m4-protractor), explicitly taught in unit 1, are retrieved on days 121 and 126. Uses unit 6's equations for angles (IM's arrow 7.6 to 7.7). Additional clusters, kept to IM's length.
8 Chance as a number from 0 to 1 (7.SP.C.5); relative frequency approaching probability (7.SP.C.6); uniform and non-uniform models, from reasoning (7.SP.C.7a) and from data (7.SP.C.7b); compound events with lists, tables and tree diagrams (introduced here), sample spaces, and designing a simulation (7.SP.C.8a to c); populations and samples, random sampling, inferences and how much estimates vary (7.SP.A.1, 7.SP.A.2); comparing two populations by center and variability as a multiple of MAD (7.SP.B.3) and by measures of center and variability of random samples (7.SP.B.4). Major work practiced: probabilities and population estimates written as percents and proportions (7.RP.A.3). Supporting and additional clusters; uses unit 4's percent and grade 6 data. IM's last unit. Course-level check 3 follows.
MATH DAYS
