g6-si-narrate | g5-si-narrate | g7-si-narrate | ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e6-present-claims (presenting in order to an audience) | Students recount a school-life event in a new middle school setting (a first week with several teachers, a club meeting) for a peer, with clear time shifts. Entry points (all recount a school-life event in middle school, a first week with several teachers or a club meeting, for a peer, with clear time shifts): levels 1 and 2 with a story map by class period, a word bank and the cloze "In first period ___. Later that day ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Before ___, I had ___. Later that day ___.", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, keeping the point of view steady across periods, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount ordered by time and place; past perfect for an earlier event ("I had already left") and pronouns kept steady (I, we, they); later that day, before and by then. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes. Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language first; the peer hears it in English, and the class word wall shows the Spanish periodo beside period. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 the settings multiply (several teachers, new routines), so time shifts between places are the new step; the grade 5 day-by-day cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.
g6-si-inform | g5-si-inform | g7-si-inform | ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e6-present-claims (telling and describing aloud) | Students explain a class routine to a new student and ask teachers clarifying questions across several classes. Entry points (all explain a class routine to a new student and ask a teacher a clarifying question in another class): levels 1 and 2 with the routine on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "In this class you ___. First ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "In this class, students ___. Could you explain ___?", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, comparing two classes' routines, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a routine in sections (arriving, working, leaving); formal register with no "you" in the written version ("students hand in"); first of all, in this class and could you explain. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea. Home language: a new student who shares a home language may hear the routine in it from a buddy first, by choice, then in English; no student is made the class interpreter. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 routines differ from class to class, so informing includes asking each teacher; the grade 5 progress cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-si-explain | g5-si-explain | g7-si-explain | ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns) | Students explain how they will meet a deadline across classes and why they planned it that way. Entry points (all explain how they will meet a deadline across classes and why they planned it that way): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a cause chain by day, a word bank and the cloze "I will ___ on ___ so that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "If I ___, then ___. That is why ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming a risk to the plan and what they will do, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a plan with its reasons and an if-then step; "if ___, then ___" and "that is why"; deadline, due and in order to. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with one unseen step (the risk). Home language: students may plan in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers here (asistir means attend, not assist). Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 the plan crosses several classes, so if-then reasoning joins the because; the grade 5 step cards are gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-si-argue | g5-si-argue | g7-si-argue | ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns) | Students take part in a structured group decision with roles, asking for and weighing reasons. Entry points (all take part in a structured group decision with roles, asking for and weighing reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and a role card, a word bank and the cloze "I think ___ because ___. What is your reason?", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Some people think ___. However, ___." and the role's question card, off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two reasons aloud before the vote, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a structured decision with roles (chair, timekeeper, note taker); "Some people think ___. However, ___" and "according to"; however, weigh and role. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a group may weigh reasons in a shared home language before speaking in English; the teacher names the cognate argumento and argument. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 roles structure the decision, so asking for a reason is a job, not a favor; the grade 5 case frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-la-narrate | g5-la-narrate | g7-la-narrate | ela:e6-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e6-plot-episodes (reading literature, same text) | Students take apart one episode of the ELA unit's novel: how the narrator's pronouns hold the point of view and the past perfect reaches back. They map the novel's episodes, then build a narrative scene with dialogue and a steady point of view, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA novel's episodes and write a scene with dialogue and a steady point of view): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of episodes, a word bank and the cloze "I ___. Before that, I had ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with point-of-view frames and transition frames ("Later that night, ___."), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining one point-of-view choice, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a scene told from one point of view; past perfect for an earlier event and point-of-view pronouns kept steady; later that night, had already and transition. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; narrador and narrator are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 point of view and episodes are the target; texts are grade 6 literature with reading support; in grade 8, the owner year, the point-of-view frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.
g6-la-inform | g5-la-inform | g7-la-inform | ela:e6-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e6-section-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e6-pronouns (forms of English) | Students take apart one section of the ELA unit text: how its heading and a classifying sentence ("can be divided into") build the central idea. They map the sections, then build an informative section with a heading, a definition and transitions, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA text's sections build the central idea and write a section with a heading, a definition and transitions): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of sections, a word bank and the cloze "The central idea is ___. One section ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "___ can be divided into ___. The first type is ___." and heading frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, keeping a formal style with no "you", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a section with a heading that builds a central idea; classifying ("types of", "can be divided into") and a formal register with no "you"; section transitions and central idea. Home language: a student may plan the section in the home language first; clasificar and classify, sección and section are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 sections and formal style are the target; texts are grade 6 with reading support; in grade 8 the classifying cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-la-argue | g5-la-argue | g7-la-argue | ela:e6-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e6-trace-argument (reading reasons and evidence) | Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the writer cites evidence with "according to" and a reporting verb. They trace the writer's argument on a claim chart, then build one argument paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace an ELA writer's argument and write a paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The writer claims ___. According to ___, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with citation frames ("The author states that ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering one counterclaim in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an argument paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence; reporting verbs (claims, states) and "according to"; cite, evidence and claims. Home language: a student may plan the argument in the home language first; citar and cite are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 cited evidence and claim charts are the target; in grade 8 the citation cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-ma-explain | g5-ma-explain | g7-ma-explain | math:m6-ratio-parts-problems (ratio problems); math:m6-one-step-equations (equations explained) | Students explain how they solved ratio problems and one-step equations in the math unit, using tables and tape diagrams. Entry points (all explain how they solved a ratio problem or a one-step equation, using a table or tape diagram): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled table or tape diagram, a word bank and the cloze "For every ___, there are ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "If ___, then ___. The variable represents ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it, with precise terms, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8, the owner year, the "for every" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explained in steps; passive voice for what is done to a number ("both sides are divided by 3") and if-then; ratio, rate and for every. Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; razón is the Spanish for ratio as well as reason, which the teacher names. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations use ratio and variable language; in grade 8 the "for every" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-ma-argue | g5-ma-argue | g7-ma-argue | math:m6-equivalent-expressions (equivalence claims); math:m6-statistical-questions (statistical questions) | Students argue whether two expressions are equivalent and whether a question is statistical, giving reasons and an example. Entry points (all argue whether two expressions are equivalent and whether a question is statistical, giving reasons and an example): levels 1 and 2 with the expressions on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "They are equivalent because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Some people think ___. However, ___. A counterexample is ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution using conditions and definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with a reason, an example and an answer to another view; "Some people think ___. However, ___" and reporting verbs; counterexample, always and sometimes. Home language: a student may test examples in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (expresión and expression, estadística and statistical). Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims are checked with examples; in grade 8 the equivalence cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-sc-explain | g5-sc-explain | g7-sc-explain | science:s6-water-cycle-energy (host investigation); science:s6-air-masses-weather (host investigation) | Students explain how energy drives the water cycle and how air masses make weather, using the science unit's models. Entry points (all explain how energy drives the water cycle and how air masses make weather, using the science unit's models): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "Water is heated, so ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "When water is heated, it ___. If ___, then ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming the mechanism and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the "is heated" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a mechanism in stages; passive voice for processes ("water is heated") and nominalization (evaporation, condensation); if, then and air mass. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; evaporación and evaporation, condensación and condensation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations name the mechanism; in grade 8 the "is heated" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g6-sc-argue | g5-sc-argue | g7-sc-argue | science:s6-plate-evidence (host investigation); science:s6-particle-temperature (host investigation) | Students argue from fossil and rock evidence that plates have moved and about how particle motion relates to temperature. Entry points (all argue from fossil and rock evidence that plates have moved, and how particle motion relates to temperature): levels 1 and 2 with the data sets on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The fossils show ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to the data, ___. Some people think ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another claim and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the "fossils show" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with a claim, data and a counterclaim answered; reporting verbs and "according to"; fossil, supports and counterclaim. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; fósil and fossil are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims use data sets; in grade 8 the "fossils show" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
