g7-si-narrate | g6-si-narrate | g8-si-narrate | ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e7-present-salient (presenting in order to an audience) | Students recount what happened in a lab or group session for a teammate who missed it, keeping each member's part straight. Entry points (all recount a lab or group session for a teammate who missed it, keeping each member's part straight): levels 1 and 2 with the group's role list on a story map, a word bank and the cloze "First ___ measured ___. Then ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "While ___ was ___, ___ had already ___.", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing varied speech verbs for what each member said, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount that tracks several people at once; past progressive with past perfect ("while she was timing, he had already poured") and pronouns kept clear; while, during the lab and my teammate. Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language first; laboratorio and lab, experimento and experiment are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 group roles and lab routines add several people to one recount, so pronouns must stay clear; the grade 6 period-by-period map is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.
g7-si-inform | g6-si-inform | g8-si-inform | ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e7-present-salient (telling and describing aloud) | Students report a lab group's plan with roles and materials and summarize another group's plan. Entry points (all report a lab group's plan with roles and materials and sum up another group's plan): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a text map with materials pictured, a word bank and the cloze "We need ___. ___ will ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The materials are ___. The roles can be divided into ___.", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up the other group's plan and naming one difference, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan report in sections (materials, roles, steps); classifying ("can be divided into") and formal register ("each member will"); materials, the roles are and in summary. Home language: a group may plan in a shared home language first; materiales and materials are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the plan has materials and roles, so classifying joins reporting; the grade 6 routine frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-si-explain | g6-si-explain | g8-si-explain | ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns) | Students explain a group's procedure and the reason for each step, and ask "why" questions of another group. Entry points (all explain the group's procedure and the reason for each step, and ask "why" of another group): levels 1 and 2 with the procedure on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "We ___ so that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The water is heated so that ___. If we skip this step, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, asking another group a "why" question about one step, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a procedure explained step by step with a reason for each; passive voice for steps ("the water is heated") and if-then for what goes wrong; so that, if we skip and procedure. Home language: groups may talk the procedure through in a shared home language first; procedimiento and procedure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the explanation is of a set procedure, so the passive voice appears; the grade 6 deadline frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-si-argue | g6-si-argue | g8-si-argue | ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns) | Students discuss a class issue in a structured debate format, building on and questioning others' reasons. Entry points (all discuss a class issue in a structured debate, building on and questioning others' reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both sides, a word bank and the cloze "I agree with ___ because ___. However ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "___ claims that ___. However, ___." and a question card, off before the end-of-unit debate; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, questioning the strongest reason on the other side, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a debate turn with a claim, a reason and a response to the other side; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) and "However, ___"; according to, claims and on the other hand. Home language: a student may prepare the turn in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates debate and debate, opinión and opinion. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the debate format means answering a named speaker's reason, so reporting verbs join; the grade 6 role card is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-la-narrate | g6-la-narrate | g8-la-narrate | ela:e7-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e7-story-elements-interact (reading literature, same text) | Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where the setting changes what a character does, and the verbs that carry the change. They trace how setting and character shape the plot, then build a scene where a setting changes what a character does, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how setting shapes a character in an ELA text and write a scene where the setting changes what a character does): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of setting and character, a word bank and the cloze "Because the ___ was ___, ___ had to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The storm had ___, so ___." and dialogue-tag frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining one choice of setting, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 point-of-view frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene where setting drives the action; past perfect for what the setting had done and varied dialogue tags; because of, had to and setting. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate tensión and tension. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 the interaction of story elements is added; in grade 8 the setting cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.
g7-la-inform | g6-la-inform | g8-la-inform | ela:e7-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e7-text-organization (reading informational, same text); ela:e7-phrases-clauses (forms of English) | Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text that classifies or compares, and the phrases and clauses that carry it ("unlike", "which means"). They map how the text is organized, then build an informative paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA text is organized and write a paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text): levels 1 and 2 with the text map, a word bank and the cloze "___ and ___ are both ___. ___ is different because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Unlike ___, ___. According to the text, ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the order of the comparison, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 classifying cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text; classifying and formal register with phrases and clauses ("unlike", "which means"); section transitions and according to the text. Home language: a student may group the comparison in the home language first; diferente and different, similar and similar are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 organization by classifying and comparing is added; in grade 8 the comparison cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-la-argue | g6-la-argue | g8-la-argue | ela:e7-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e7-evaluate-argument (reading reasons and evidence) | Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and judge whether its evidence is enough, marking the reporting verbs and "however". They then build one paragraph that answers a counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all judge whether an ELA text's evidence is enough and write a paragraph that answers a counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of claim and evidence, a word bank and the cloze "Some people think ___. However, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The evidence is (not) enough because ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, judging sufficiency in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 citation cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that judges evidence and answers a counterclaim; "Some people think ___. However, ___" and reporting verbs; enough, however and some argue. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; suficiente and sufficient are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 a counterclaim is added; in grade 8 the "Some people think" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-ma-explain | g6-ma-explain | g8-ma-explain | math:m7-proportional-equations (proportional equations); math:m7-two-step-equations (two-step equations) | Students explain how they wrote and solved proportional and two-step equations from the math unit, saying what each part means. Entry points (all explain how they wrote and solved a proportional or two-step equation, saying what each part means): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled equation and tape diagram, a word bank and the cloze "___ represents ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "First ___ is subtracted from both sides. If ___, then ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining what each symbol represents, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "for every" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that names what each symbol stands for; passive voice for steps and if-then; proportional, constant and represents. Home language: a student may talk through the equation in the home language first; proporcional and proportional, constante and constant are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 students explain what each symbol represents; in grade 8 the "represents" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-ma-argue | g6-ma-argue | g8-ma-argue | math:m7-triangle-conditions (triangle conditions); math:m7-compare-populations (comparing samples) | Students argue whether three lengths make a triangle and compare two populations from samples, citing the data. Entry points (all argue whether three lengths make a triangle and compare two populations from samples, citing the data): levels 1 and 2 with the strips or data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "They make a triangle because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to the data, ___. However, ___." and a condition frame ("This works only if ___."), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution using conditions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 counterexample cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from a condition or from data, with another view answered; reporting verbs and "according to the data"; condition, never and sample. Home language: a student may test the lengths in the home language first; condición and condition, población and population are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 conditions and data are the evidence; in grade 8 the triangle cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-sc-explain | g6-sc-explain | g8-sc-explain | science:s7-photosynthesis (host investigation); science:s7-cell-respiration-growth (host investigation) | Students explain photosynthesis and how cells use food for energy and growth, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain photosynthesis and how cells use food for energy and growth): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "Light is used to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Carbon dioxide is taken in and ___. If ___, then ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the unseen step and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "is heated" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that follows matter and energy through a process; passive voice ("is converted", "is taken in") and nominalization (photosynthesis, respiration); if, then and glucose. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; fotosíntesis and photosynthesis, glucosa and glucose are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 explanations follow matter and energy; in grade 8 the "light is used" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
g7-sc-argue | g6-sc-argue | g8-sc-argue | science:s7-ecosystem-disruption (host investigation); science:s7-body-systems-food (host investigation) | Students argue how a change to an ecosystem affects its populations and how body systems work together, from evidence. Entry points (all argue how a change to an ecosystem affects its populations and how body systems work together, from evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "When ___ changed, the population ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, linking evidence to claim with reasoning and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 "fossils show" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with reasoning that links evidence to claim; reporting verbs and "Some argue that ___. However, ___"; population, argues and reasoning. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; ecosistema and ecosystem are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 reasoning that links evidence to claim is the target; in grade 8 the population cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.
