### Where the threads end

Grade 12 is the last ELD year. Every content thread ends in the owner year's unit (28 to 36), checked by a task the
student does alone at levels 3 and 4 from the model with no sentence frame (the row notes' grade 12 withdrawal).
The four SI threads end here too, carried since their owner year, grade 4. The school's own exit score, not this
course, decides when a student leaves English learner status.



1. **The C11 gate holds the later stages.** This frame is written under Cory's 2026-11-25 call (DESK-ANSWERS, after-core frames); the scope and sequence, unit plans, days and newcomer strand of all four ELD courses wait (the scope and sequence part of COURSE-RUN stage 3, and stages 4 to 6) until C11 passes in the probe and guard units of two core subjects, one of them ELA (STANDARD section 6).
2. **Ids checked against the framework pages (pilot pattern note).** The owner-year parts named in units 28 to 36 are our reading of the WIDA 2020 framework's 9-12 lists, from the progression rows and memory: the framework PDF (wida.wisc.edu, WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf, the 9-12 expectations pages) was not opened, since this job allows no downloads. Pick: the frame reader or the fix pass checks each of the 18 ids at the printed pages and edits the "every part of the ids" sentences in place.
3. **Several hosts left out where a row names two.** Units 1, 4, 10, 14, 19, 21, 22, 24 and 29 use one of their row's two hosts because the other is taught later in the year (for example g9-sc-argue's common ancestry, g10-sc-explain's reaction rates, g11-sc-explain's wave technologies). Pick: keep the rows as written (the ids are met on one host, and the cross-subject tables say which is left out); no row change. The alternative, a year order that reaches every second host, would put three or four units in each of the last ten weeks.
4. **Grade 12 hosts across elective courses.** Each grade 12 MA and SC unit sets one shared host and lets each student apply the language to their own course (The course in brief, "Grade 12 hosts"). Pick: keep this; the scope and sequence writes the per-course method and claim cards. A class where most seniors take no math or science course in grade 12 runs the fallback rows' grade 11 hosts for all.
5. **Senior calendar.** If seniors leave more than 5 days early, pick: the school places the 160 course days so they end on the seniors' last day (CALENDAR.md), rather than cutting unit 36 further.
6. **After-core check weeks among themselves.** The eight checks sit in grade 9, 10 and 11 week 17, grade 12 week 16, and week 32 in every grade; kept as they are for the lead's calendar pass across the after-core frames (CALENDAR.md rule 1).
7. **The seam below.** ELD grades 6 to 8 is being written at the same time. Each grade 9 unit retrieves its grade 8 row; once that frame exists, its grade 8 unit numbers go into the Picked up table and the Readiness bullet (the Pending marker there).
8. **Newcomer strand time (people and time, Cory's call, as in the pilot).** The high school strand meets students who often arrive mid-year and with interrupted schooling; who teaches the side table, and whether the school gives a second ELD period, are Cory's to settle before the strand is written.


