10 | 13 | ss7-roman-republic; ss7-republic-to-empire; ss7-roman-slavery-class | Rome on a map and its growth to about 500 CE, and how its location supported its power; the Republic's consuls, senate, assemblies, tribunes, veto, Twelve Tables, separation of powers and civic duty, matched to grade 5's branches-of-government model; the Republic's crisis (land and the Gracchi, armies loyal to their generals, civil wars) and how Caesar and Augustus turned the Republic into an empire, and why the empire grew and lasted, with one concrete case of military tactics and organization (the legion) linked to conquest; Rome's classes and slavery (conquest, birth, debt; households, farms, mines; Spartacus; freeing and its limits), from sources where enslaved and freed people's voices survive. MA.7.T4c.3a (the legion, and decentralized rule through provinces and local elites) is taught here; MA.7.T4c.3b and MA.7.T4c.3c (the empire's economy) are taught and owned in unit 11.
11 | 11 | ss7-roman-economy; ss7-roman-engineering; ss7-latin-legacy | What Roman taxes paid for and who paid; one currency, roads and safe sea lanes under the Pax Romana; customs duties and who gained and lost; inflation when silver was cut from coins, and the 33 CE credit crisis with falling land prices, set against an illustrative case of general deflation; who is hurt by inflation, by deflation and by unemployment, each compared separately for different groups (personal finance touch, no added day: why savings kept as coins lose value when prices rise) (cases labeled illustrative where the record is thin); roads, aqueducts, the arch and concrete; Latin and Greek roots in school and science words.
12 | 12 | ss7-rome-fall; ss7-ideas-spread | Internal and external causes of the Western empire's breakup by 476, while the Eastern empire lasted another thousand years; a multi-cause argument that takes a side in the historians' debate (a fall or a slower transformation), and names the strongest counterclaim; how ideas moved across Europe, Asia and Africa by trade, migration, conquest and colonization, on one world map. Christianity's growth in the late empire (Constantine, then a state religion) returns as review of grade 6 unit 7, as evidence in the transformation debate.

